[{:db/id 17592186142809 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cb3897a4-5231-57eb-ab41-23374e791687" "question-216167a3-fd82-5cdc-8020-0d45fc502fbb" "question-6df23865-1abd-55c3-8027-8e081f02fcc6" "question-a38d8b59-e40a-56ad-8fcf-e14dfa4ff5e1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142620 :question/problem "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n![](images/9f/9f7b4224da0b74247411a42ff7786defaa871f790874dc9f7e8b390f769ea65c.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 1)+1}{2}= \\dfrac {3} {2}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {7} {3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{3}{3}\\times \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =1\\times \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{7}}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142810 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f66c5704-c44a-54d2-8737-7a35f6074418" "question-99bb9dc4-abe2-5180-ad2c-f6dd7e678c81" "question-1c726818-6cc1-5342-8e08-a605c7092b6e" "question-ce672d7a-cb19-5ec5-9614-ee57ab637b77" "question-177fabe4-7f4b-5692-9ebe-916f1e142f79"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142621 :question/problem "What is the value of $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} ?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 1)+1}{2}= \\dfrac {3} {2}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $1\\,\\dfrac 1{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{3}{3}\\times \\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =1\\times 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} = 2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142811 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8325b264-7339-533f-b5e7-72d4a3741a6e" "question-948afd53-5fc4-5fae-b3e0-fa8392f07e57" "question-f0192ebd-c39a-5e07-8c9d-bdc0f4e15605" "question-c3d8799e-4d03-502b-a27c-a344247e81c1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142623 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $3\\,\\dfrac 1{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}= \\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $1\\,\\dfrac 2 {5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 2 {5}=\\dfrac{(1\\times 5)+2}{5}= \\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 {2} \\times \\dfrac{7}{5} =  \\dfrac {7\\times 7} {2\\times 5} = \\dfrac{49}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{49}{10} =4\\,\\textrm{R} 9 = 4\\,\\dfrac 9 {10}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142812 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d2626f9e-5300-5e18-a1f2-581c7a661b16" "question-107d23c7-53ab-5ba0-9a97-cca159b8c473" "question-3cce7163-7034-5329-8f1c-2dea6d81db92" "question-14bd50e2-6db5-5d60-b6e7-3a96bb6d176e" "question-867d7685-55ae-5d57-a541-e247084d254e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142625 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {7} {3}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $2\\,\\dfrac 1{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}= \\dfrac {9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{7}{4}\\times \\frac{9}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\times 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{21}{4} = 5\\,\\textrm{R} 1= 5\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186143583 :question/problem "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\times 2\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{5}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{(1\\times 5)+3}{5}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+3}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}\\times \\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}\\times \\frac{11}{4} & =\\frac{8}{4}\\times \\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =2\\times \\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $22.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142465 :answer-field/correct 17592186142469 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-cb3897a4-5231-57eb-ab41-23374e791687" :question/id #uuid "cb3897a4-5231-57eb-ab41-23374e791687" :question/math-academy-id "q-57139" :question/problem "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times 1\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{4}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(1\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3\\times 3}{2\\times 2}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-367db085-cbf2-562d-8300-b1b60aa8064a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-367db085-cbf2-562d-8300-b1b60aa8064a" :answer-field/id #uuid "367db085-cbf2-562d-8300-b1b60aa8064a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-51ca6f24-fd38-5631-a70f-1fed4a5df934" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4439ba27-ef63-593e-8a22-d9a840edb673" "answer-afa464f5-b1c4-5dc3-a1d9-d0e30709123f" "answer-fafdf436-ad85-5cc9-9732-253546e28802" "answer-7a915a0f-513b-5fc9-a71f-e578f2edf1f2" "answer-51ca6f24-fd38-5631-a70f-1fed4a5df934"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4439ba27-ef63-593e-8a22-d9a840edb673" :answer/id #uuid "4439ba27-ef63-593e-8a22-d9a840edb673" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afa464f5-b1c4-5dc3-a1d9-d0e30709123f" :answer/id #uuid "afa464f5-b1c4-5dc3-a1d9-d0e30709123f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fafdf436-ad85-5cc9-9732-253546e28802" :answer/id #uuid "fafdf436-ad85-5cc9-9732-253546e28802" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a915a0f-513b-5fc9-a71f-e578f2edf1f2" :answer/id #uuid "7a915a0f-513b-5fc9-a71f-e578f2edf1f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51ca6f24-fd38-5631-a70f-1fed4a5df934" :answer/id #uuid "51ca6f24-fd38-5631-a70f-1fed4a5df934" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-216167a3-fd82-5cdc-8020-0d45fc502fbb" :question/id #uuid "216167a3-fd82-5cdc-8020-0d45fc502fbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-57146" :question/problem "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\times 1\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{15}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{(2\\times 5)+1}{5}=\\frac{11}{5}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{11\\times 4}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{44}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $44.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-db3af647-a25b-5f37-b07f-1f150d912b72"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-db3af647-a25b-5f37-b07f-1f150d912b72" :answer-field/id #uuid "db3af647-a25b-5f37-b07f-1f150d912b72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-777a1e79-8a56-5593-8bfe-18320c8d2e0d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c60bce2c-8440-55e5-afc8-f10ffbd555be" "answer-8d87ae66-66cd-5754-85bd-8a6bebf0652a" "answer-d8e21707-2e75-58f0-b256-742a1e9989b6" "answer-4b2a54f8-a470-5d7a-b6ae-afdd72170abe" "answer-777a1e79-8a56-5593-8bfe-18320c8d2e0d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c60bce2c-8440-55e5-afc8-f10ffbd555be" :answer/id #uuid "c60bce2c-8440-55e5-afc8-f10ffbd555be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d87ae66-66cd-5754-85bd-8a6bebf0652a" :answer/id #uuid "8d87ae66-66cd-5754-85bd-8a6bebf0652a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8e21707-2e75-58f0-b256-742a1e9989b6" :answer/id #uuid "d8e21707-2e75-58f0-b256-742a1e9989b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b2a54f8-a470-5d7a-b6ae-afdd72170abe" :answer/id #uuid "4b2a54f8-a470-5d7a-b6ae-afdd72170abe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-777a1e79-8a56-5593-8bfe-18320c8d2e0d" :answer/id #uuid "777a1e79-8a56-5593-8bfe-18320c8d2e0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "44" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6df23865-1abd-55c3-8027-8e081f02fcc6" :question/id #uuid "6df23865-1abd-55c3-8027-8e081f02fcc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-137143" :question/problem "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times 1\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{8}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(4\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{(1\\times 4)+3}{4}=\\frac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{2}\\times \\frac{7}{4}=\\frac{9\\times 7}{2\\times 4}=\\frac{63}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $63.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-26e43b54-c17c-50b5-9239-7e57f9be7377"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-26e43b54-c17c-50b5-9239-7e57f9be7377" :answer-field/id #uuid "26e43b54-c17c-50b5-9239-7e57f9be7377" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-64c5e584-b4a2-5a1f-ae34-c9993d849468" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c6a72ef-6e83-5f6d-9226-b960c62dd1ec" "answer-c0402afe-da0a-50bb-9387-6d3f57d0bcb9" "answer-64c5e584-b4a2-5a1f-ae34-c9993d849468" "answer-e1641237-a5a5-57fb-9916-0b3083f4b4b1" "answer-4636236c-c1ff-57a9-864c-319255aa568f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c6a72ef-6e83-5f6d-9226-b960c62dd1ec" :answer/id #uuid "3c6a72ef-6e8